      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by expressing both sides with the same base. Notice that we can rewrite all the bases as powers of 7 or powers of 8 (or in this case, both 7 and 8).\n\nFirst, we isolate the powers of 7 on one side of the equation by subtracting 7 from both sides:\n\\[\n7^{x+7} = 8^x \\implies 7^{x+7-7} = 8^x \\implies 7^x = 8^x.\n\\]\nSince the bases are not directly equal, we take the natural logarithm of both sides to handle the exponents:\n\\[\n\\ln(7^x) = \\ln(8^x).\n\\]\nUsing the logarithm power rule \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we get:\n\\[\nx \\ln(7) = x \\ln(8).\n\\]\nSince \\(x\\) is not equal to zero, we can divide both sides by \\(x\\) (noting that \\(x\\) is not zero):\n\\[\n\\ln(7) = \\ln(8).\n\\]\nThis equation implies that the arguments of the logarithms must be equal:\n\\[\n7 = 8.\n\\]\nHowever, this is not correct, so we need to re-evaluate the initial assumption that \\(x = 0\\). Instead, we solve the equation by isolating \\(x\\) directly:\n\\[\n\\ln(7^x) = \\ln(8^x) \\implies x \\ln(7) = x \\ln(8) \\implies x (\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)) = 0.\n\\]\nSince \\(x \\neq 0\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\ln(7) - \\ln(8) = 0 \\implies \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right) = 0.\n\\]\nThis can only be true if \\(\\frac{7}{8}\\) is 1, which is not possible. However, we can substitute back to find \\(x\\):\n\\[\n\\ln(7) = \\ln(8) \\implies x = \\log_b 7^7.\n\\]\nSince the bases are the same, we can equate the arguments:\n\\[\n7^x = 8^x \\implies x = \\log_{\\frac{7}{8}} (7^7).\n\\]\nThis implies we need to find \\(b = \\frac{7}{8}\\). So the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{8}}\n\\]",
